    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`, and it now behaves the way you described. The existing tests pass (3 of 3), and I also tried all three cases against a real in-process MCP server.\n\n```python\nasync def run_tool(session, name, arguments):\n    result = await session.call_tool(name, arguments)\n    if result.is_error:\n        raise ToolCallFailed(name, _text(result))\n    if result.structured_content is not None:\n        return result.structured_content\n    return _text(result)\n```\n\n`_text` joins the text blocks with `\"\\n\"` and skips everything else, such as images or resources.\n\nThe code uses the attribute names from the installed mcp 2.2.0 (`is_error`, `structured_content`), which I looked up rather than guessed. Structured output counts as present whenever it isn't `None`, so values like `{}`, `[]` or `0` are returned as-is and not treated as missing.\n\n**Results from the real-server check:**\n- **Structured output:** `{'service': 'api', 'up': True}` came back as a dict, and an empty `{}` came back as `{}`, not text.\n- **Text only:** `'hi'`.\n- **Failed tool:** it raised `ToolCallFailed('boom', 'Error executing tool boom')`.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **The error message is whatever the server sends.** The SDK's own `MCPServer` hides the original exception (\"no such version\") and sends only a generic \"Error executing tool \u2026\", so that's what `ToolCallFailed.message` holds. Your ops server may send more detail.\n- **Some tools won't return structured output.** With this SDK version, a server tool annotated as a bare `-> dict` sends only JSON text. `run_tool` then correctly returns that text, which the CLI prints as a string.\n- **Cleanup needed:** I couldn't delete my scratch script `_e2e_check.py` in the project root because the `rm` needed approval. Please delete it; pytest won't pick it up."
